# React Native and Expo

Write clean, readable JavaScript with functional components and hooks. Keep each component focused on one responsibility, and group related components, hooks, and styles by feature (`user-profile`, `chat-screen`).

## Naming

- Variables and functions: camelCase (`isFetchingData`, `handleUserInput`)
- Components: PascalCase (`UserProfile`, `ChatScreen`)
- Directories: lowercase and hyphenated (`user-profile`, `chat-screen`)

## JavaScript

- Avoid global variables
- Prefer ES6+: arrow functions, destructuring, and template literals
- Use PropTypes for component props when the file is not TypeScript

## Performance

- Keep state local and skip updates that do not change the UI
- Wrap pure functional components in `React.memo`
- Tune `FlatList` with `removeClippedSubviews`, `maxToRenderPerBatch`, and `windowSize`
- Do not pass inline anonymous functions to `renderItem` or event handlers

## UI

- Define styles with `StyleSheet.create()`, or Styled Components when styles depend on props
- Adapt layout to screen size and orientation (`react-native-responsive-screen` or similar)
- Load images with an optimized library such as `react-native-fast-image`

## Expo

- Respect React Native's threading model so the UI thread stays responsive
- Ship with EAS Build and EAS Update for continuous deployment and OTA updates
- Use Expo Router for file-based routing, native navigation, and deep linking on Android, iOS, and web: https://docs.expo.dev/router/introduction/

## Example

```jsx
const OrderRow = React.memo(function OrderRow({ item, onPress }) {
  return (
    <Pressable onPress={onPress} style={styles.row}>
      <Text>{item.name}</Text>
    </Pressable>
  );
});

OrderRow.propTypes = {
  item: PropTypes.shape({ name: PropTypes.string }).isRequired,
  onPress: PropTypes.func.isRequired,
};
```

Pass a stable `onPress` from the parent. Define it outside `renderItem`.
